'id'=>'variables','name'=>'Variables','icon'=>'📦','color'=>'#6366f1',
'subs'=>[
['name'=>'Declaration & Naming','brief'=>'Create and name variables','explanation'=>'PHP variables start with a dollar sign ($) followed by the name. Variable names are case-sensitive and must start with a letter or underscore, followed by letters, numbers, or underscores. PHP is loosely typed so you do not need to declare a data type. Variables can be reassigned to different types at any time.','syntax'=>'<span class="cmt">// Basic variable declaration</span>\n<span class="var">$name</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"John"</span>;\n<span class="var">$age</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">25</span>;\n<span class="var">$is_student</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">true</span>;\n<span class="var">$pi</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">3.14</span>;\n\n<span class="cmt">// Dynamic typing</span>\n<span class="var">$var</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"hello"</span>;\n<span class="var">$var</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">42</span>;\n<span class="var">$var</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">true</span>;','codeExample'=>'<span class="op">&lt;?</span><span class="kw">php</span>\n<span class="var">$name</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"Alice"</span>;\n<span class="var">$age</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">28</span>;\n<span class="var">$email</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"alice@example.com"</span>;\n<span class="var">$height</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">5.6</span>;\n<span class="var">$is_active</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">true</span>;\n\n<span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="str">"Name: </span><span class="var">$name</span><span class="str">&lt;br&gt;"</span>;\n<span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="str">"Age: </span><span class="var">$age</span><span class="str">&lt;br&gt;"</span>;\n<span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="str">"Type: "</span> . <span class="fn">gettype</span>(<span class="var">$name</span>);','useCases'=>['Storing user input from forms','Holding calculation results','Managing state in a script','Passing data between functions'],'mistakes'=>['Using reserved keywords as variable names','Starting variable names with numbers','Forgetting the $ prefix when accessing variables'],'tips'=>['Use meaningful, descriptive variable names','Follow PSR naming: camelCase for variables','Use var_dump() or gettype() to inspect types'],'interview'=>['What are the rules for naming PHP variables?','Is PHP strongly or weakly typed?','What happens if you use an undefined variable?']],
['name'=>'Variable Scope','brief'=>'Local, global, and static scope','explanation'=>'Variable scope determines where a variable can be accessed. Local variables are declared inside a function and cannot be accessed outside it. Global variables must be declared global inside a function using the global keyword. Static variables retain their value between function calls.','syntax'=>'<span class="cmt">// Local scope</span>\n<span class="kw">function</span> <span class="fn">test</span>() {\n    <span class="var">$local</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"I am local"</span>;\n}\n\n<span class="cmt">// Global scope</span>\n<span class="var">$globalVar</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"I am global"</span>;\n\n<span class="kw">function</span> <span class="fn">test2</span>() {\n    <span class="kw">global</span> <span class="var">$globalVar</span>;\n    <span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="var">$globalVar</span>;\n}\n\n<span class="cmt">// Static scope</span>\n<span class="kw">function</span> <span class="fn">counter</span>() {\n    <span class="kw">static</span> <span class="var">$count</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">0</span>;\n    <span class="var">$count</span>++;\n    <span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="var">$count</span>;\n}','codeExample'=>'<span class="op">&lt;?</span><span class="kw">php</span>\n<span class="var">$greeting</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"Hello World"</span>;\n\n<span class="kw">function</span> <span class="fn">sayHello</span>() {\n    <span class="kw">global</span> <span class="var">$greeting</span>;\n    <span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="var">$greeting</span> . <span class="str">"&lt;br&gt;"</span>;\n}\n\n<span class="kw">function</span> <span class="fn">increment</span>() {\n    <span class="kw">static</span> <span class="var">$count</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">0</span>;\n    <span class="var">$count</span>++;\n    <span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="str">"Call #</span><span class="var">$count</span><span class="str">&lt;br&gt;"</span>;\n}\n\n<span class="fn">sayHello</span>();\n<span class="fn">increment</span>();\n<span class="fn">increment</span>();\n<span class="fn">increment</span>();','useCases'=>['Counting function calls with static','Sharing data between functions using global','Encapsulating logic within functions','Maintaining state across calls'],'mistakes'=>['Forgetting global keyword inside functions','Overusing global variables','Not understanding static vs global'],'tips'=>['Avoid globals - pass data as parameters','Use static for counters within functions','Consider classes instead of global state'],'interview'=>['What is the difference between global and static?','Can local variables access globals directly?','How do you access a global inside a function?']],
['name'=>'Superglobals','brief'=>'Built-in global arrays','explanation'=>'PHP provides built-in superglobal arrays that are always accessible regardless of scope. These include $_GET, $_POST, $_SERVER, $_FILES, $_COOKIE, $_SESSION, and $_ENV. They contain information about the request, server environment, and sessions.','syntax'=>'<span class="cmt">// $_GET - URL parameters</span>\n<span class="var">$_GET</span>[<span class="str">\'key\'</span>]\n\n<span class="cmt">// $_POST - Form data</span>\n<span class="var">$_POST</span>[<span class="str">\'key\'</span>]\n\n<span class="cmt">// $_SERVER - Server info</span>\n<span class="var">$_SERVER</span>[<span class="str">\'REQUEST_METHOD\'</span>]\n\n<span class="cmt">// $_SESSION - Session data</span>\n<span class="var">$_SESSION</span>[<span class="str">\'user_id\'</span>]','codeExample'=>'<span class="op">&lt;?</span><span class="kw">php</span>\nsession_start();\n<span class="var">$page</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="var">$_GET</span>[<span class="str">\'page\'</span>] <span class="op">??</span> <span class="num">1</span>;\n<span class="var">$username</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="var">$_POST</span>[<span class="str">\'username\'</span>] <span class="op">??</span> <span class="str">\'\'</span>;\n<span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="var">$_SERVER</span>[<span class="str">\'REQUEST_METHOD\'</span>];\n<span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="var">$_SERVER</span>[<span class="str">\'REMOTE_ADDR\'</span>];\n<span class="var">$_SESSION</span>[<span class="str">\'logged_in\'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">true</span>;\n<span class="var">$_SESSION</span>[<span class="str">\'user_id\'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">42</span>;','useCases'=>['Reading form submissions ($_POST)','Getting URL parameters ($_GET)','Accessing server info ($_SERVER)','Managing sessions ($_SESSION)'],'mistakes'=>['Not checking if key exists before using','Trusting superglobal data without sanitization','Forgetting session_start() before $_SESSION'],'tips'=>['Always validate and sanitize superglobal data','Use null coalescing (??) with superglobals','Check REQUEST_METHOD before processing forms'],'interview'=>['What are PHP superglobals? List 5.','What is the difference between $_GET and $_REQUEST?','Why never trust data from $_GET directly?']],
['name'=>'Variable Variables','brief'=>'Dynamic variable names','explanation'=>'Variable variables let you use the value of a variable as the name of another variable using the $$ syntax. The value of $var becomes the name of the variable accessed through $$var. Use it sparingly for readability.','syntax'=>'<span class="var">$varName</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">\'hello\'</span>;\n<span class="cmt">// Creates variable $hello</span>\n<span class="var">$$varName</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"world"</span>;\n<span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="var">$hello</span>;  <span class="cmt">// world</span>\n<span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="var">$$varName</span>; <span class="cmt">// world</span>','codeExample'=>'<span class="op">&lt;?</span><span class="kw">php</span>\n<span class="var">$fruit1</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"Apple"</span>;\n<span class="var">$fruit2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"Banana"</span>;\n<span class="var">$fruit3</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"Cherry"</span>;\n\n<span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="var">$i</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">1</span>; <span class="var">$i</span> <span class="op">&lt;=</span> <span class="num">3</span>; <span class="var">$i</span>++) {\n    <span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="str">"</span><span class="op">{</span><span class="str">\'fruit\'</span> <span class="op">.</span> <span class="var">$i</span><span class="op">}</span><span class="str">: </span><span class="op">${</span><span class="str">\'fruit\'</span> <span class="op">.</span> <span class="var">$i</span><span class="op">}</span><span class="str">&lt;br&gt;"</span>;\n}\n\n<span class="var">$name</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">\'age\'</span>;\n<span class="op">${</span><span class="var">$name</span><span class="op">}</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">25</span>;\n<span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="var">$age</span>; <span class="cmt">// 25</span>','useCases'=>['Dynamic form field processing','Creating variables in a loop','Dynamic configuration loading','Template variable substitution'],'mistakes'=>['Using when an array would be simpler','Making code unreadable','Not understanding case sensitivity'],'tips'=>['Prefer arrays over variable variables','Use ${"varName"} syntax for clarity','Only use when there is a clear benefit'],'interview'=>['What are variable variables in PHP?','When are arrays better than variable variables?','How do you access a variable variable?']],
['name'=>'References','brief'=>'Assign by reference with &','explanation'=>'References allow two variables to refer to the same data. When you assign by reference using &, both variables point to the same memory location. Changes to one affect the other. Useful for passing large data without copying.','syntax'=>'<span class="var">$a</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">10</span>;\n<span class="var">$b</span> <span class="op">=&</span> <span class="var">$a</span>;\n<span class="var">$b</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">20</span>;\n<span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="var">$a</span>; <span class="cmt">// 20</span>\n\n<span class="kw">function</span> <span class="fn">increment</span>(<span class="op">&</span><span class="var">$num</span>) {\n    <span class="var">$num</span>++;\n}','codeExample'=>'<span class="op">&lt;?</span><span class="kw">php</span>\n<span class="var">$original</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"Hello"</span>;\n<span class="var">$ref</span> <span class="op">=&</span> <span class="var">$original</span>;\n<span class="var">$ref</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="str">"Modified"</span>;\n<span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="var">$original</span>; <span class="cmt">// Modified</span>\n\n<span class="kw">function</span> <span class="fn">swap</span>(<span class="op">&</span><span class="var">$a</span>, <span class="op">&</span><span class="var">$b</span>) {\n    <span class="var">$temp</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="var">$a</span>;\n    <span class="var">$a</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="var">$b</span>;\n    <span class="var">$b</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="var">$temp</span>;\n}\n<span class="var">$x</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">1</span>; <span class="var">$y</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="num">2</span>;\n<span class="fn">swap</span>(<span class="var">$x</span>, <span class="var">$y</span>);\n<span class="kw">echo</span> <span class="str">"</span><span class="var">$x</span><span class="str">, </span><span class="var">$y</span><span class="str">"</span>; <span class="cmt">// 2, 1</span>','useCases'=>['Swapping two variable values','Modifying variables inside functions','Passing large arrays efficiently','Building linked data structures'],'mistakes'=>['Accidentally creating references','Using references in foreach incorrectly','Not understanding objects pass by handle'],'tips'=>['Use references only when needed','Unset &$var in foreach after loop','Objects already pass by handle in PHP'],'interview'=>['What is the difference between assignment and reference?','How do you pass by reference to a function?','Do objects need references in PHP?']]
